  "TITLE": "Allosteric Regulation:<br>Feedback Without Touching<br>the Active Site",
  "LEAD": "Allosteric regulation lets a molecule change an enzyme's activity by binding a completely different site than the one where the reaction actually happens, twisting the enzyme's shape just enough to speed it up or shut it down. It's the same logic behind feedback control in a plant: a downstream sensor &mdash; a composition analyzer, a discharge pressure transmitter &mdash; throttles an upstream valve or compressor without ever touching the reaction itself, using the process's own output to govern its input.",
  "CHART_TITLE": "END PRODUCT FEEDS BACK TO AN UPSTREAM ENZYME",
